 a diagonal are now covered by a single sunny line), we have the relation  \n\n     \\[\n     k_2 \\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\\qquad\n     k_1 = k-2k_2 .\n     \\]\n\n   - Choose \\(k_2\\) distinct interior diagonals \\(D_{c}\\) with \\(c\\in\\{3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and, on each, pick any two of its points. Replace each such diagonal by the sunny line through the chosen pair (Lemma\u202f3).  \n   - If \\(k\\) is odd we still have to replace one more diagonal; we use one of the two extreme diagonals \\(D_2\\) or \\(D_{n+1}\\) and replace it by a sunny line through its unique point (Lemma\u202f2).  \n   - After performing the above replacements we have removed exactly \\(k\\) diagonals and added exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, so the total number of lines remains \\(n\\).\n\n6. **Verification of the covering condition.**  \n   Every point of \\(T_n\\) lies either  \n   * on a diagonal that has not been removed, or  \n   * on a sunny line that was introduced to replace the diagonal containing that point.  \n   Hence all points are covered.\n\n7. **Optimality.**  \n   At most \\(n\\) of the \\(n\\) lines can be sunny, so no integer \\(k>n\\) is possible. The construction above shows that each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Diagonal covering)\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(D_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_{\\text{all}}=\\{D_c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\).  The set \\(\\mathcal D_{\\text{all}}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, all of which have slope \\(-1\\) and are therefore **not** sunny.  Hence the value \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Isolated sunny line through a prescribed point)\n\nLet \\(P=(p_x,p_y)\\in T_n\\).  \nThere exists a line \\(L\\) through \\(P\\) whose slope \\(\\lambda\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\n\\]\n\nand such that \\(L\\cap T_n=\\{P\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider only non\u2011vertical lines; a vertical line has slope \\(\\infty\\) and is forbidden.  \nFor any finite slope \\(\\lambda\\neq -1\\) the line through \\(P\\) is  \n\n\\[\nL_\\lambda:\\; y-p_y=\\lambda(x-p_x). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(L_\\lambda\\) also passes through another point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\setminus\\{P\\}\\), then from (1)\n\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\frac{b-p_y}{a-p_x}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus each distinct extra point of \\(T_n\\) determines a single rational value of \\(\\lambda\\).  \nSince \\(T_n\\) is finite, the set  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\Bigl\\{\\frac{b-p_y}{a-p_x}\\;\\Big|\\;(a,b)\\in T_n,\\;(a,b)\\neq P\\Bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nis finite.  \n\nAdd the two slopes that are explicitly forbidden for a sunny line:\n\n\\[\nS' = S\\cup\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S'\\) is still a finite subset of \\(\\mathbb Q\\).  \nBecause the rational numbers are infinite, we can choose a rational number  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda\\in\\mathbb Q\\setminus S'.\n\\]\n\nSuch a \\(\\lambda\\) is automatically different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); it is finite, so the corresponding line \\(L_\\lambda\\) is not vertical (hence its slope is not \\(\\infty\\)).  \n\nBy construction \\(\\lambda\\notin S\\), therefore (2) cannot hold for any \\((a,b)\\neq P\\) in \\(T_n\\). Consequently the line \\(L_\\lambda\\) meets \\(T_n\\) only at \\(P\\).  \n\nFinally, the slope \\(\\lambda\\) avoids the three prohibited values, so \\(L_\\lambda\\) is sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Sunny line through two points of the same diagonal)\n\nLet \\(c\\) satisfy \\(3\\le c\\le n\\).  \nPick any two distinct points \\(P,Q\\in T_n\\) with \\(P_x+P_y=Q_x+Q_y=c\\).  \nThe line through \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\mu=\\frac{Q_y-P_y}{\\,Q_x-P_x\\,}\\neq -1,\n\\]\n\nhence it is sunny.  Moreover it contains exactly the two chosen points of \\(T_n\\) (no other point of \\(T_n\\) can lie on the same line, because any third point would have the same sum \\(c\\) and would be collinear with \\(P,Q\\), forcing the three points to be collinear with slope \\(-1\\), a contradiction). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\nk = 2t + \\varepsilon ,\\qquad t=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\\; \\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(t\\) is the number of *double\u2011point* replacements we shall perform and \\(\\varepsilon\\) indicates whether we need one additional *single\u2011point* replacement.\n\n1. **Double\u2011point replacements.**  \n   Choose any \\(t\\) distinct interior diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{c_1},D_{c_2},\\dots ,D_{c_t},\\qquad 3\\le c_i\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n   On each \\(D_{c_i}\\) pick two distinct points of \\(T_n\\); by Lemma\u202f3 the line through the two points is sunny and covers exactly those two points.  \n   Replace each diagonal \\(D_{c_i}\\) by the corresponding sunny line.  \n   After this step we have removed \\(t\\) diagonals and added \\(t\\) sunny lines; the total number of lines is still \\(n\\).\n\n2. **Single\u2011point replacement (if \\(\\varepsilon=1\\)).**  \n   The two extreme diagonals \\(D_2\\) and \\(D_{n+1}\\) each contain exactly one point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Choose one of them, say \\(D_2\\), and apply Lemma\u202f2 to its unique point; we obtain a sunny line that contains that point and no other point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Replace \\(D_2\\) by this sunny line.  \n   This removes one more diagonal and adds one more sunny line, preserving the total number \\(n\\).\n\n3. **The remaining diagonals.**  \n   All diagonals that have not been removed stay in the family; they are non\u2011sunny and together with the newly introduced sunny lines form a set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\bigl\\{ \\text{all untouched } D_c \\bigr\\}\\cup\n               \\bigl\\{ \\text{sunny lines from steps\u202f1 and\u202f2} \\bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The cardinality of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal L| = n - t - \\varepsilon + t + \\varepsilon = n .\n   \\]\n\n   Exactly \\(k=2t+\\varepsilon\\) of its members are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered\n\n* Points that lie on a diagonal that was **not** removed are covered by that diagonal.  \n* For each interior diagonal \\(D_{c_i}\\) that was removed, the two points we selected on it are covered by the sunny line that replaced the diagonal (Lemma\u202f3).  \n* If a single\u2011point diagonal \\(D_2\\) (or \\(D_{n+1}\\)) was removed, its unique point is covered by the sunny line supplied by Lemma\u202f2.\n\nThus every element of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Optimality\n\nA family of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, so no integer \\(k>n\\) can be realised.  \nThe construction above shows that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
