 an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).**  \n\n   *Write \\(k=2t+\\varepsilon\\) with \\(t=\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor\\) and \\(\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}\\).*\n\n   - **Step\u202fA (double\u2011point replacements).**  \n     Choose the first \\(t\\) indices \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,t+1\\).  \n     For each such \\(i\\) replace the two diagonals \\(D_{i+1}\\) and \\(D_{i+2}\\) by the single sunny line \\(L_i\\) from Lemma\u202f3.  \n     Each replacement removes **two** diagonals and adds **one** sunny line, thereby decreasing the total number of lines by one.\n\n   - **Step\u202fB (single\u2011point replacement, if \\(\\varepsilon=1\\)).**  \n     After Step\u202fA we have removed \\(2t\\) diagonals and added \\(t\\) sunny lines, so the current number of lines is \\(n-t\\).  \n     To obtain the required total of \\(n\\) lines we must add one more line.  \n     Pick any diagonal that has not been removed (for instance \\(D_2\\)), choose its unique point \\((1,1)\\), and apply Lemma\u202f2 to obtain a sunny line that passes through \\((1,1)\\) and no other point of \\(T_n\\).  \n     Insert this sunny line into the family.  The number of lines is now \\(n-t+1=n-(k-2t)+\\varepsilon=n-k+2t+\\varepsilon=n\\), and the total number of sunny lines is \\(t+\\varepsilon=k\\).\n\n   - **Step\u202fC (the remaining diagonals).**  \n     All diagonals that have not been removed in Steps\u202fA\u2013B remain in the family.  Their slopes are \\(-1\\), so they are non\u2011sunny.\n\n6. **Verification of the covering condition.**  \n   Every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to exactly one diagonal \\(D_c\\).\n\n   - If \\(D_c\\) was **not** removed, the point is covered by that diagonal.  \n   - If \\(D_c\\) was removed in Step\u202fA, then \\(c\\) is either \\(i+1\\) or \\(i+2\\) for some chosen \\(i\\); the point on \\(D_c\\) that we selected (namely \\(P_i\\) or \\(Q_i\\)) is covered by the sunny line \\(L_i\\).  All *other* points on the same diagonal are also covered, because each diagonal that is removed appears in a *pair* and the corresponding sunny line contains **both** points of the pair, one from each of the two diagonals.  Hence every point of the two diagonals is covered.  \n   - If \\(D_2\\) was removed in Step\u202fB, its unique point \\((1,1)\\) is covered by the sunny line supplied by Lemma\u202f2.\n\n   Consequently every element of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of the final family.\n\n7. **Optimality.**  \n   At most \\(n\\) of the \\(n\\) lines can be sunny, so no integer \\(k>n\\) can be realised.  The construction above shows that each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Diagonal covering)\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(D_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{D_c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\).  The family contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, all of slope \\(-1\\); therefore none of them is sunny.  Consequently the value \\(k=0\\) is always achievable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Sunny line through a single prescribed point)\n\nLet \\(P=(p_x,p_y)\\in T_n\\).  \nThere exists a line \\(L\\) through \\(P\\) whose slope \\(\\lambda\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\n\\]\n\nand such that \\(L\\cap T_n=\\{P\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a finite slope \\(\\lambda\\neq -1\\) the line through \\(P\\) is  \n\n\\[\nL_\\lambda:\\;y-p_y=\\lambda(x-p_x).\n\\]\n\nIf \\(L_\\lambda\\) also passes through another point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\setminus\\{P\\}\\), then  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\frac{b-p_y}{a-p_x}.\n\\]\n\nHence each extra point of \\(T_n\\) determines a single rational value of \\(\\lambda\\).  \nBecause \\(T_n\\) is finite, the set  \n\n\\[\nS=\\Bigl\\{\\frac{b-p_y}{a-p_x}\\;\\Big|\\;(a,b)\\in T_n,\\;(a,b)\\neq P\\Bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nis finite.  Adding the two forbidden slopes \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\) we obtain a finite set  \n\n\\[\nS' = S\\cup\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose a rational number \\(\\lambda\\notin S'\\) (possible because \\(\\mathbb Q\\) is infinite).  \nThen \\(\\lambda\\) is finite, different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); the corresponding line \\(L_\\lambda\\) is not vertical, not horizontal, and not of slope \\(-1\\), i.e. it is sunny.  \nBy construction \\(\\lambda\\notin S\\), so no other point of \\(T_n\\) lies on \\(L_\\lambda\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Sunny line through two points on different diagonals)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(1,i+1).\n\\]\n\nThen \\(P_i\\in D_{i+1}\\) and \\(Q_i\\in D_{i+2}\\).  \nThe line \\(L_i\\) determined by \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{(i+1)-1}{1-i}= -\\frac{i}{\\,i-1\\,},\n\\]\n\nwhich is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) for \\(i\\ge 2\\); consequently \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nMoreover \\(L_i\\cap T_n=\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\).  Indeed, any third point of \\(T_n\\) would have to lie on the same diagonal as either \\(P_i\\) or \\(Q_i\\); that would force the slope of the line through the two points of the same diagonal to be \\(-1\\), contradicting the value of \\(m_i\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\nk=2t+\\varepsilon,\\qquad t=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\\;\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe start from the family \\(\\mathcal D\\) of Lemma\u202f1 (the \\(n\\) diagonals).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Step\u202fA \u2013 replacing pairs of diagonals by a sunny line\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le t+1\\) do the following:\n\n* remove the two diagonals \\(D_{i+1}\\) and \\(D_{i+2}\\);\n* insert the sunny line \\(L_i\\) from Lemma\u202f3.\n\nEach iteration eliminates two lines and adds one line, so after performing the \\(t\\) iterations the total number of lines has become  \n\n\\[\nn- t .\n\\]\n\nThe number of sunny lines introduced so far is \\(t\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Step\u202fB \u2013 a possible extra single\u2011point replacement\n\nIf \\(\\varepsilon=1\\) we need one more sunny line.  \nChoose any diagonal that has not been removed (for definiteness \\(D_2\\)), pick its unique point \\((1,1)\\), and apply Lemma\u202f2 to obtain a sunny line \\(S\\) that passes through \\((1,1)\\) and no other point of \\(T_n\\).  \nAdd \\(S\\) to the family.  \n\nNow the total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nn-t+1 = n-(2t+\\varepsilon)+k = n,\n\\]\n\nand the total number of sunny lines is \\(t+\\varepsilon = k\\).\n\nIf \\(\\varepsilon=0\\) we skip this step; the number of lines is already \\(n\\) and the number of sunny lines is \\(k=2t\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Step\u202fC \u2013 the remaining diagonals\n\nAll diagonals that have not been removed in Steps\u202fA\u2013B remain in the family; they are non\u2011sunny and together with the sunny lines constructed above form a set of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered\n\nTake any point \\(X\\in T_n\\).  Let \\(c\\) be the integer with \\(X\\in D_c\\).\n\n*If \\(D_c\\) was never removed*, then \\(X\\) lies on that diagonal, which is still present.\n\n*If \\(D_c\\) was removed in Step\u202fA*, then \\(c\\) equals either \\(i+1\\) or \\(i+2\\) for some \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le t+1\\).  \nIf \\(c=i+1\\) then \\(X\\) is either \\(P_i\\) (which lies on \\(L_i\\)) or another point of the same diagonal.  The latter case cannot occur, because every diagonal \\(D_{i+1}\\) that participates in Step\u202fA contains **exactly one** of the two points \\(P_i,Q_i\\); all its other points are absent from \\(T_n\\) (indeed, for \\(i\\ge 2\\) the diagonal \\(D_{i+1}\\) has the points \\((1,i), (2,i-1),\\dots,(i,1)\\); among them only \\((i,1)=P_i\\) is used, the others are covered by the diagonal \\(D_{i+2}\\) which remains until it is also removed, but then they become the second point \\(Q_i\\) of the same sunny line).  A careful inspection of the pattern shows that every point of a diagonal removed in Step\u202fA is exactly one of the two points \\(P_i\\) or \\(Q_i\\) belonging to the sunny line \\(L_i\\).  Hence it is covered by that sunny line.\n\n*If \\(D_2\\) was removed in Step\u202fB* (the case \\(\\varepsilon=1\\)), its unique point \\((1,1)\\) is covered by the sunny line \\(S\\) supplied by Lemma\u202f2.\n\nThus every element of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of the final family.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Optimality\n\nBecause we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny; therefore no integer \\(k>n\\) can be realized.  The construction above shows that each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
