aims that the only points of such a diagonal that belong to \\(T_n\\) are the two special points \\(P_i\\) or \\(Q_i\\). This is **false**.  \n\n    Example: take \\(i=3\\). The diagonal \\(D_{i+1}=D_4\\) consists of the points \\((1,3), (2,2), (3,1)\\). In the construction, \\(P_3=(3,1)\\) is indeed on the sunny line \\(L_3\\), but the other points \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) are **not** on any sunny line \\(L_j\\) (they are not \\(Q_j\\) for any \\(j\\)) and the diagonal \\(D_4\\) has been removed, so they are uncovered. The same phenomenon occurs for every diagonal \\(D_{i+1}\\) with \\(i\\ge2\\): each contains the whole set \\(\\{(1,i), (2,i-1),\\dots,(i,1)\\}\\); only the endpoint \\((i,1)=P_i\\) is covered by \\(L_i\\), while the other points are left without a line.  \n\n    Consequently the claim that “every point of a diagonal removed in Step A is exactly one of the two points \\(P_i\\) or \\(Q_i\\)” is incorrect, and the covering property fails for any \\(k>0\\). This breaks the entire construction and invalidates the conclusion that all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable.\n\n- Because this error occurs in the core verification of the construction, the solution does not establish the existence of the required families of lines for \\(k>0\\).\n\n#### Optimality argument\n- The statement “at most \\(n\\) lines can be sunny” is trivial and correct, but it is irrelevant since the existence part is already false.\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion:** The solution’s construction for \\(k>0\\) does not actually cover all points of \\(T_n\\); the covering argument contains a critical logical flaw. Therefore the solution is invalid."
